First, to summarize the problem:

When you have hundreds of pages (< totalItems/pageSize >→ many hundreds) the page‐picker `<Select>` renders an unbounded dropdown of every single page, which quickly overflows the browser and is a terrible UX.

### Root cause

In `packages/react/src/components/Pagination/Pagination.js`, the “page number” picker is **always** rendered as a `<Select>` with N `<SelectItem>`s, one for every page up to `totalPages`.  When `totalPages` is large, that huge dropdown is unusable.

### Fix

Instead of always using a `<Select>`, switch to a native `<input type="number">` (or any other Number/Text Input component) for the page number.  This allows the user to simply type “60” (or “158”) rather than scroll through hundreds of options.

Below is a *SEARCH/REPLACE* patch against your existing `packages/react/src/components/Pagination/Pagination.js`.  It replaces the right‐hand `<Select>` block with a `<input type="number">` field.  No new imports are needed.

```javascript
### packages/react/src/components/Pagination/Pagination.js
<<<<<<< SEARCH
          <Select
            id={`${prefix}-pagination-select-${inputId}-right`}
            className={`${prefix}--select__page-number`}
            labelText={`Page number, of ${totalPages} pages`}
            inline
            hideLabel
            onChange={this.handlePageInputChange}
            value={statePage}
            disabled={pageInputDisabled}>
            {selectItems}
          </Select>
=======
          <input
